#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#. [set_menu_item]: id=sw_field_manual
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:329
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:345
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:340
msgid "Field Manual"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:346
msgid "Which section of the field manual?"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:347
msgid "The Force and ysalamiri"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:347
msgid ""
"Force users carry Force Points, shown as blue pips beside them. They regain a few each turn. Right-click a Force user and choose <b>The Force…</b> to spend them: Force Push and Pull move an enemy one hex, Force Speed adds movement, Force Sense reveals every living enemy within 4 hexes until your next turn, even hidden Noghri or enemies in fog, and marks presences up to 8 hexes away, Mind Trick dazes a weak-minded enemy for a turn, and Force Choke is the dark side's weapon. Blaster Deflection works by itself, turning aside overwatch fire for one Force Point.\n"
"\n"
"A ysalamiri pushes the Force back in a bubble around itself, shown as violet hexes. A Force user inside the bubble shows a violet slashed ring: no powers, no deflection, no regeneration, and no Force agility or healing. Nothing inside the bubble can be targeted by a Force power either. A carried ysalamiri moves its bubble with its bearer."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:350
msgid "Overwatch"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:350
msgid ""
"Right-click a soldier and choose <b>Overwatch</b> to give up this turn's attack and watch instead. A gold crosshair marks the unit, with one bar per reaction left. Until its next turn, an enemy that moves within its range and in plain sight draws reaction fire. The moving unit stops when fired on but keeps its remaining moves.\n"
"\n"
"Hidden units cannot be targeted. Each watcher fires at most once per enemy move order. Overwatch ends at the start of its side's next turn. Enemy units hold overwatch too when they end a turn without attacking."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:353
msgid "Blaster fire"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:353
msgid ""
"The colour of a bolt tells you who is shooting: red for the Imperial Remnant and other dark forces, blue or green for the New Republic and its allies, orange for independent smugglers.\n"
"\n"
"Some craft keep their own colours: TIE starfighters and Star Destroyer turbolasers fire green, and X-wing, Y-wing and A-wing lasers fire red. Stun settings fire blue rings, and ion cannons fire a pale blue discharge that slows what it hits."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:356
msgid "Space battles"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:356
msgid ""
"Open space can only be crossed by starfighters and capital ships; ground troops stay aboard. Asteroid fields cost starfighters extra movement but give them cover, and capital ships cannot enter them.\n"
"\n"
"New squadrons launch from hangar bays and launch rails, which work like a keep and castle."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:359
msgid "Bases and settlements"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:359
msgid ""
"Command bunkers and shuttle landing pads are keeps: a leader standing on one can call in reinforcements onto the surrounding bunker aprons or landing aprons.\n"
"\n"
"Settlements, supply depots, Wookiee dwellings and Noghri dukhas count as villages: they heal units and earn credits each turn for whoever holds them. Bulkheads cannot be crossed."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:362
msgid "Heroes"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:362
msgid "Named heroes cannot be replaced. If one of the heroes named in a mission's objectives falls, the mission is lost. Heroes who survive continue to the next mission."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:363
msgid "Stealth and hidden hazards"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:363
msgid ""
"Noghri commandos are invisible in forest or at night, unless an enemy with <i>keen senses</i> is within two hexes of them. They are revealed when an enemy moves next to them.\n"
"\n"
"Cloaked asteroids stay hidden until your sensors identify them or something runs into them, and a ship that ends its turn next to one takes damage, though never enough to destroy it. Sensors cannot track living stealth such as the Noghri's; Force Sense and keen senses can."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:366
msgid "Sensors and cloaking"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:366
msgid ""
"Your sensors build a picture of enemies you cannot see. An amber dashed hex with a question mark is an unknown contact. An amber hex with a symbol is classified by type: capital ship (wedge), starfighter (X), vehicle, infantry, creature, or object. A red hex with corner brackets is identified but out of sight. Red brackets around a visible ship mean you have burned through its cloak. Right-click a marked hex and choose <b>Sensor contact</b> to see the strength of the reading and what it is made of.\n"
"\n"
"Sensor strength is the observer's sensors, minus 1 for every 2 hexes of distance, minus enemy jamming, plus the target's signature, minus its cloak and cover. Asteroid fields, forests, settlements and mountains give cover. A reading of 0 is a contact, 3 classifies it, and 6 identifies it. Being next to a unit always identifies it. A cloaked unit stays hidden until your team identifies it, and its cloak drops for a turn when it fires.\n"
"\n"
"Units with active sensors can make an <b>Active sensor sweep</b> instead of attacking: until their next turn they add their sweep rating and see 2 hexes further, but they become easier to detect. Jammers (ECM, yellow waves) weaken enemy sensors near them and near what they protect, but are loud themselves; counter-jamming (ECCM) cancels jamming. Some ships launch <b>sensor decoys</b> that read as real contacts until enemy sensors see through them or a unit comes next to them. Treat unconfirmed contacts with suspicion."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:371
msgid ""
"Starfighters support ground battles from off the map. When your side has sorties, right-click a hex that one of your forward observers can see (or tracks on sensors) within range and choose <b>Call air support</b>. Forward observers are commanders such as Han, Leia and Lando, sergeants, commandos and scouts; calling uses the observer's attack, and only one sortie flies per turn.\n"
"\n"
"A <b>strafing run</b> strikes at once along four hexes leading away from the observer: three shots of 6 at every unit there. A <b>bombing run</b> marks the target and the six hexes around it with orange circles and lands at the start of your next turn: two bombs of 9 at every unit there, with +10% accuracy. Sorties hit friend and foe alike, so mind your own troops. Each shot's chance to hit depends on the target's terrain cover; enemy anti-air within range lowers accuracy by 20% each (40% at most), and enemy jamming over the target lowers it by 10% per point. Sorties never finish off a unit: they soften, your troops finish. The enemy may have sorties too; get out of a marked area before it lands."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:374
msgid "Ranks"
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:374
msgid "Veterans wear their rank. A unit that rises past its highest level gains a rank — Veteran, Seasoned veteran, Elite — shown as insignia on the unit: gold chevrons for the New Republic, the red-and-blue rank plaque for the Imperial Remnant, brass studs for independents, violet marks for dark Jedi. Promoted units such as sergeants and smuggler veterans start at the first rank. Each rank also brings the usual +3 maximum hit points and a full heal."
msgstr ""

#. [message]: speaker=narrator
#: utils/hte_macros.cfg:375
msgid ""
"Grand Admiral Thrawn learns his enemies. His forces gain Insight from what they observe: how you move, the weapons you use, the targets you choose, your reinforcements, your formations and your tactics. Repeating yourself teaches him faster. Captured art, archives and intelligence can teach him too. Red pips over his commander show how far his doctrine has come.\n"
"\n"
"At set levels of Insight his forces gain, in order: an intelligence summary, pattern recognition, sensor doctrine (better identification and counter-jamming, and more decoys seen through), prediction of the units you are likely to attack next, and coordinated fire (+10% to hit the kinds of unit he has studied in action). Right-click his commander and choose <b>Tactical status</b> to see his current Insight and active effects. Insight never shows him what his sensors cannot detect, so cloaks and jamming still work against him. Vary your tactics."
msgstr ""

